{
  "platform_id": "VkQLrAPUzWM",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:VkQLrAPUzWM",
    "purpose": "Compare five winter down jackets/parkas (Filson, The North Face, Patagonia, Arc'teryx and Manifattura Ceccarelli), explain how down works, and give an informal cold-weather shiver test for each.",
    "video_type": "comparison",
    "thesis": "Down jackets differ mainly in construction philosophy, technical waterproof shells versus heritage waxed cotton or wool. There is no single winner. The right jacket depends on use and activity level, and layering correctly matters most.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Down is not feathers. Quality down jackets are mostly down, and 80/20 down-to-feather is roughly the lowest ratio worth buying.",
      "Fill power is warmth-to-weight efficiency, while down weight is the amount of down. Lower fill power dries faster than higher fill power.",
      "Fill powers covered: Filson Down Cruiser 650 (80/20), North Face McMurdo 600, Patagonia Tres 700, Arc'teryx 750, Manifattura Ceccarelli 800.",
      "Informal time-to-shiver at about 5°F: Filson 1:52, McMurdo 6:12, Patagonia 2:02, Arc'teryx 4:45. He did not shiver in the Ceccarelli.",
      "Of these brands, only Arc'teryx uses Gore-Tex. North Face and Patagonia use their own proprietary waterproof membranes.",
      "Arc'teryx's down composite mapping and Contour construction add synthetic insulation in wet-prone areas. Synthetic insulation compresses over time, which can leave cold spots.",
      "Waterproof membrane jackets can delaminate over time. Waxed cotton heritage jackets patina instead.",
      "Activity level and correct layering matter more than the jacket itself."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Video introduction",
        "summary": "Previews the five coats and the shiver test, and describes the Huckberry duck boots and waxed leather mittens he is wearing.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Filson Down Cruiser",
        "summary": "Construction of the Filson Down Cruiser: Shelter Cloth arms, lighter cover cloth upper, merino-lined collar and pockets, and why waxed cotton protects untreated down.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Understanding down fill",
        "summary": "Explains down versus feathers, down/feather ratios, fill power and down weight, and drying speed. Then gives the Filson's specs (650 fill, 80/20), mentions another Filson down coat, and reports its shiver time.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "North Face McMurdo",
        "summary": "Covers the McMurdo's proprietary waterproof shell, 600 fill down, hand warmer pockets, Velcro storm flap, hood with removable fur trim, and the drafts through the hood snaps, plus its shiver time.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Patagonia 3-in-1 parka",
        "summary": "Explains why the Patagonia Tres 3-in-1 parka disappointed him: a good rain shell and good fit, but compromised as a winter jacket. Covers its 700 fill down, unlined parka length and weak pockets.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Arc'teryx technology",
        "summary": "Covers Arc'teryx's philosophy of using cutting-edge technology with no low-end line, its use of Gore-Tex, micro seaming, and an introduction to down composite mapping and Contour construction, with 750 fill and its shiver time.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Manifattura Ceccarelli",
        "summary": "Presents his Manifattura Ceccarelli fisherman down jacket: 800 fill, heavy down weight, vegetable-tanned adjusters, wool or waxed cotton outer, and the maker's history with Filson.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "Final jacket comparisons",
        "summary": "Compares technical and heritage jackets, covering delamination and the drawbacks of Arc'teryx's synthetic mapping. Groups down jacket types and stresses choosing by use and activity, with layering as the key to warmth.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 14
      }
    ],
